<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Urnes Stave Church</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1130</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Urnes Stave Church is a wooden church on the Lustrafjord, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sognefjord">Sognefjord</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, built around 1130 and among the oldest surviving stave churches. It reuses carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> from an earlier church whose interlaced animal ornament gave its name to the Urnes style, the last phase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> art. It was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church.jpg" alt="Urnes Stave Church" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leo-setä · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urnes_Stave_Church_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church, organised under the archbishopric of Nidaros (Trondheim) from 1152–1153, built stone cathedrals and stave churches such as <b>Urnes</b> and Borgund and became the largest landowner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Art historians name a sequence of styles after find-places, Oseberg, Borre, Jelling, Mammen, Ringerike and Urnes, running from about 800 into the 12th century, when the carved portal of the <b>Urnes Stave Church</b> in Norway shows the last of them.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>It became part of the Kingdom of Norway; in the Middle Ages Sogn was known for its stave churches, including <b>Urnes</b>, now a World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/state/sogn">Sogn</a></li></ul></section>
